-NOTES = r"""
-Definition and paper objects. Section 2.1, Definition 1 defines a PSD
-factorization by exact trace products. Section 6.3 defines the binary inner
-product communication matrix IP_n(x,y)=x^T y mod 2. Theorems 50 and 51 give
-the upper bound, with Theorem 51 explicitly constructing a low-rank signed
-square root and hence rank-one real PSD factors. This module remains in those
-objects: a matrix B specifies W_B(x,y)=x^T B y, and a checked BQ=I transports
-the paper's factorization by the column relabelling y=Qz.
-
-Step-0 discrimination. Directly generating arbitrary PSD factors would not
-justify Track A. PSD-factorization is an existential-real search problem, and
-exact low-rank matrix signing has dedicated algorithms; in particular, fixed
-rank is polynomial-time and generic instances are FPT in rank. More directly
-for this family, its witness is always produced by ordinary Gaussian
-elimination in polynomial time. The claim is therefore Track B, never Track A.
-The mechanical route is dense GF(2) Gauss-Jordan elimination. The compact route
-notices that complementing the public dense matrix exposes disjoint 3-by-3
-blocks and applies the rank-one inverse update locally.
-
-Generation. A small invertible 3-by-3 block and its inverse are sampled
-together from a fixed library, independently relabelled, and composed into a
-block diagonal D. Blocks are chosen so 1^T D^{-1} 1=0 in GF(2). Therefore
-B=D+11^T is invertible and
-B^{-1}=D^{-1}+(D^{-1}1)(1^T D^{-1})
-by the rank-one inverse identity in characteristic two. The generator evaluates
-that identity and carries both B and B^{-1} through independent row and column
-permutations. It never runs elimination on B.
-
-Easy regimes and attacks. Section 6.3 itself gives the IP_n factorization once
-the coordinates are aligned, which is why an exposed identity transform would
-be trivial. Dense rank-one camouflage and independent coordinate relabellings
-hide the local blocks. The tests include row/column outlier matchings, greedy
-nearest-unit columns, transposition, random invertible guesses, and the strong
-in-context near miss that correctly inverts every revealed block but omits the
-rank-one correction. Exact Gauss-Jordan is intentionally successful and is
-reported separately as the Track-B reference algorithm.
-""".strip()
-
